    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The 50% promotional discount applies to the entire matrix at the time of writing. Standard rates are 2x promotional across the board. For buyers who want predictable long-term pricing, the standard rate is the more honest comparison: $4.95/mo for the entry tier, $14.95/mo for 2GB, $28.95/mo for 4GB. For buyers who can lock in the promotional rate and renew strategically, the floor is $2.48/mo for 512MB / 1 vCPU / 20GB NVMe / 1TB transfer. <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a>'s $10.98/yr annual special undercuts Cloudzy's promo floor on a per-year basis (RackNerd ≈ $0.92/mo effective for entry tier, Cloudzy promo $2.48/mo), but RackNerd is DDR4 + 1 Gbps + US-only — Cloudzy's pricing premium buys the modern infrastructure + multi-DC + crypto billing bundle.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">What Makes Cloudzy Different: Self-Funded 18-Year Track Record</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The most underappreciated feature of Cloudzy is its <strong>operating history and capital structure</strong>. In the 2026 cloud market, the dominant pattern is VC-funded "hyperscaler challenger" providers that raise hundreds of millions in funding, scale aggressively, and then either get acquired (DigitalOcean was famously public but has spent a decade trying to grow into its valuation), pivot their business model, or shut down unprofitable product lines to satisfy quarterly board pressure. Cloudzy is the opposite: self-funded since 2008, no outside capital, no acquisition, no quarterly board to satisfy. The About page says it explicitly: "No VCs, no quarterly board pressure to gouge customers on egress."
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      What this means in practice: Cloudzy's pricing decisions can be made on multi-year horizons, the 40 Gbps network upgrade wasn't tied to a funding round, the 50% promotional discount isn't a loss-leader to chase market share, and the 18-year operational continuity is a real (not marketing-claimed) track record. The 122,000+ customer count is cumulative over 18 years, not a one-quarter marketing snapshot. The 4.6/5 Trustpilot rating reflects organic review accumulation rather than a recent push.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      This is meaningfully different from a 2-year-old startup with $50M in VC funding that could pivot, get acquired, or shut down within 24 months. For buyers prioritizing operational continuity over absolute cheapest price, this is a real differentiator that does not show up in any feature comparison table.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Modern Infrastructure: DDR5 + EPYC + 40 Gbps</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Cloudzy's infrastructure story is uniformly modern across every tier. There are no legacy tiers, no SATA SSD storage, no DDR4 memory anywhere in the matrix. The Regular CPU line runs on AMD EPYC cores (the specific generation is not publicly disclosed per tier, but the published benchmarks and the DDR5 + NVMe + 40 Gbps combination place them at EPYC Milan or newer). The High-Frequency line runs on AMD Ryzen cores clocked at 4.2 GHz+, which is targeted at game server admins and trading bot operators who need high single-thread performance.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The 40 Gbps network uplink is shared per node, not per VM — but it's present on every tier, including the $2.48/mo entry. Most budget VPS providers cap at 1 Gbps shared per node, which means a single heavy network user on a 1 Gbps node can saturate the link for everyone. Cloudzy's 40 Gbps shared uplink means even a saturated 10-VM node leaves 30+ Gbps headroom, which is meaningful for environments with many concurrent small transfers (CDN nodes, torrent seedboxes, build farms, container orchestration). The pure NVMe storage is a similar story: no SATA SSD tiers, no hybrid HDD/SSD, no tiered performance — every plan gets the same NVMe-backed storage class.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Datacenter Locations and Routing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Cloudzy operates 13 active datacenters with a 14th (Tokyo) announced as "coming soon". The footprint is broad but not hyperscaler-broad (Vultr has 32+, AWS has 30+ in core regions). For most buyers, 13 nodes across NA, EU, and APAC is sufficient:
    </p>
