    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      For buyers needing EU jurisdiction, Amsterdam, Frankfurt, London, and Milan are the four EU options. For buyers wanting non-US, non-EU jurisdiction, Cloudzy is itself registered in UAE Free Zone — useful for niche buyers who want to keep data out of the US CLOUD Act and EU GDPR jurisdiction simultaneously. For APAC, Singapore and Sydney are the current nodes; Tokyo will add a third when it launches.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Payment Methods and Money-Back</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Cloudzy accepts a wide range of payment methods, with the privacy configuration being the differentiator:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
      <li><strong>Credit cards</strong> — Visa, Mastercard, AmEx, Discover. Standard processor, GDPR-driven identity verification for high-value transactions.</li>
      <li><strong>PayPal</strong> — available, with PayPal's typical account-level metadata footprint.</li>
      <li><strong>Bitcoin (BTC)</strong> — accepted directly; on-chain pseudonymous, no KYC.</li>
      <li><strong>Monero (XMR)</strong> — accepted directly; cryptographic privacy by default, sender + receiver + amount obscured on-chain. This is the meaningful differentiator for privacy-respecting buyers.</li>
      <li><strong>Tether (USDT)</strong> — accepted directly; stablecoin, no FX exposure for USD-pegged buyers.</li>
      <li><strong>Wire transfer</strong> — available for annual contracts over $1,000.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The 14-day money-back guarantee is unconditional — "no questions asked, full refund", per Cloudzy's published terms. This is meaningfully better than the typical prorated or feature-limited money-back policies that some providers use. For buyers who want to test Cloudzy's actual performance (single-thread CPU, NVMe throughput, network latency) before committing to a 12-month cycle, the 14-day window is sufficient.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">What Cloudzy Is Not</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Three things Cloudzy is <em>not</em>, that buyers sometimes assume it is:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
      <li><strong>Not the absolute cheapest VPS.</strong> <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a>'s $10.98/yr annual special is ~3x cheaper than Cloudzy's promo floor on per-year cost ($0.92/mo effective vs $2.48/mo promo). RackNerd is DDR4 + 1 Gbps + US-only; Cloudzy is DDR5 + 40 Gbps + 13 DCs. The pitch is "modern infrastructure at competitive price", not "absolute rock-bottom".</li>
      <li><strong>Not a managed services provider.</strong> There is no managed tier — the customer is responsible for OS setup, security patching, backups, application deployment, and monitoring. Cloudzy runs the hardware and the network; everything above the hypervisor is the customer's job. If you want a managed layer on top of VPS, look at Cloudways (managed WordPress/WooCommerce on AWS/DO/Vultr) or a dedicated managed provider.</li>
      <li><strong>Not a hyperscaler substitute.</strong> No managed databases, no object storage (S3-compatible), no Kubernetes, no auto-scaling groups, no CDN, no serverless functions. Cloudzy is bare VPS plus a 1000+ app marketplace. If your workload needs managed Postgres, managed Redis, or object storage, look at Vultr (managed DBs + object storage), DigitalOcean (managed DBs + Spaces), or hyperscalers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Who Should Choose Cloudzy</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Cloudzy is the right pick for a narrow but durable set of buyer profiles:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
      <li><strong>Developers needing modern infrastructure</strong> at budget prices — DDR5 + EPYC + NVMe + 40 Gbps is the future-proof combination, and Cloudzy is the cheapest place to get it.</li>
      <li><strong>Game server admins and trading bot operators</strong> — the High-Frequency AMD Ryzen 4.2 GHz+ line is targeted at single-thread-heavy workloads, with prices starting at $3.48/mo for 1GB.</li>
      <li><strong>Buyers wanting self-funded 18-year operator</strong> — Cloudzy is the cleanest match in the 2026 market for buyers who specifically want an operator that has not raised VC funding and has not been acquired.</li>
      <li><strong>Multi-DC deployments needing NA + EU + APAC</strong> — 13 active datacenters cover the three core regions from a single provider, with Tokyo coming soon as a 14th.</li>
      <li><strong>Privacy-respecting buyers wanting Monero (XMR)</strong> — direct XMR acceptance is rare for an 18-year-old cloud provider, and the no-threshold flow preserves the privacy configuration that third-party crypto processors destroy.</li>
      <li><strong>Buyers wanting UAE Free Zone jurisdiction</strong> — Cloudzy is registered in UAE, not US or EU. For buyers who want to keep data out of both CLOUD Act and GDPR jurisdiction simultaneously, this is one of the few options in the market.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Cloudzy is <em>not</em> the right pick for buyers who need managed services, GPU instances other than the L4/L40S/H100 line (which is a separate Cloudzy product), the absolute cheapest $/GB, or hyperscaler-grade 99.99%+ SLAs. Those profiles have better answers in the comparison table below.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Three Close Substitutes</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Three providers on this site that occupy adjacent positions to Cloudzy:
    </p>
    <ol class="text-gray-300 space-y-3 mb-4 ml-6 list-decimal">
      <li><strong><a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a></strong> — $10.98/yr annual entry (cheaper than Cloudzy's $2.48/mo on per-year cost at promo rates), 8 US/EU datacenters. Closer to the budget tier; less emphasis on DDR5 + 40 Gbps + multi-DC. Best for absolute cheapest. <a href="/blog/racknerd-deep-review-2025" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd review</a>.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a></strong> — $24/mo for 4GB tier (vs Cloudzy $14.48/mo promo), 32+ datacenters incl. Tokyo/Singapore/Sydney. Hyperscaler-grade managed DBs + object storage; more expensive but broader feature set. Best for managed services + region count.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/vps/hostinger" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Hostinger</a></strong> — $4.99/mo entry (managed WordPress + KVM VPS line), broader product portfolio (shared + managed WP + VPS). Less emphasis on raw EPYC + 40 Gbps; more on managed panel experience. Best for buyers wanting managed services + panel integration.</li>
    </ol>

